{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"churchy-chatty-foxterrier","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nNo code-review Pass-1 overlay owns correctness races (the security overlay covers attacker-framed TOCTOU only; performance covers load-shaped concurrency only). The traced corpus on the parent epic shows the modal class in real code is the \"unlocked twin\": a new accessor of a shared resource that bypasses the serialization discipline the resource's sibling accessors use (`eclectic-spotted-barb`, `malevolent-emigratory-umbrette`, `grassgreen-clotty-duck`), followed by check-then-act TOCTOU (`fine-wisteria-solenodon`).\n\n## Approach\n\nAuthor `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-concurrency.md` following the established Pass-1 specialist overlay pattern — frontmatter, concerns-to-flag, bright-line rule, false-positive guards, and the evidence-record contract all carried IN the overlay because the Pass-2 verifier is domain-blind (pattern: `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-security.md`, `code-review-performance.md`). The rubric, decided on the parent epic:\n\n1. **Concurrency-model gate first**: name the concurrency source that executes the path (threads, async tasks, multiprocessing, multiple processes/replicas, detached children, signal handlers) or PASS as not-applicable — concurrency you cannot name is not a finding.\n2. **Lockset / unlocked-twin check, first-class**: flag a new accessor of a shared resource that does not follow the serialization/atomicity discipline the resource's existing accessors use — the finding must cite the sibling accessor and its guard.\n3. **The race quartet as the bright line** — every finding names all four: the shared mutable resource (quoted), ≥2 concurrent executors (spawn/entry point cited), the interleaving window (quoted read + quoted write; includes two independent reads of mutable external state assumed equal — HEAD, mtime, a directory listing), and a concrete harm (lost update, double-processing, corrupted invariant, deadlock — \"could behave unexpectedly\" is not a harm).\n4. **FP guards**, phrased structurally so less capable agents can apply them: already-serialized (a `with lock:`/transaction/atomic single statement/CAS/version check around the window — verified in code, not assumed); not-actually-shared (function-local, per-request, thread-local); unreachable concurrency (import-time, run-once); benign-tolerant race (the value is only logged/metrics, never branched on); **disposition-aware guard suppression** — suppress only when the guard PREVENTS the harm; a guard that detects the condition and proceeds anyway is itself a finding; defer BY NAME: attacker-controlled TOCTOU on a security boundary → `security`; concurrency whose only harm is load → `performance`.\n5. Evidence-record contract, `criteria: [\"concurrency\"]`, no model-emitted severity, empty findings expected.\n\nPositive rationale: the lockset heuristic (Eraser 1997; Meta's RacerD, deployed at scale) is the highest-precision race signal in production static analysis, and it is a citation LOOKUP rather than an interleaving argument — so it stays reliable on less capable agents, and each quartet element decomposes into a sub-claim the existing Pass-2 verifier disciplines already check (cited-reference accuracy, reachability-via-callers, prove-absence-by-searching), keeping validity scoring functional instead of drowning race findings in `insufficient`. Explicitly excluded from the rubric (weak-agent hazard, decided): memory-model/GIL/single-statement-atomicity reasoning and idempotency-under-retry judgment.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-concurrency.md` (new file; the reviewers directory is the packaged prompt source).\n\n## Testing\n\nThis is an LLM prompt surface: behavioral validation is a standing eval with pinned model and seeded recall/no-fire diffs, delivered by the dependent sibling eval story (linked `depends_on` this ticket; alias recorded there and in this ticket's comments once created). In-session checks here are structural: the frontmatter parses and carries the required keys, and the rubric sections named in the acceptance criteria are present in the committed file (structural greps are the legitimate oracle for a document artifact).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-concurrency.md` exists with frontmatter mirroring `code-review-security.md`: `outputs: code_review_findings`, `execution_mode: agentic`, `category: code-review-pass`, `dimension: code-review-concurrency`, a `langfuse_prompt` key; checked by reading the file.\n- [ ] The rubric contains, verifiable by grep on the committed file: the concurrency-model gate, the unlocked-twin/lockset check citing \"sibling\" discipline, all four quartet elements, the disposition-aware guard-suppression rule, and defer-by-name lines for `security` and `performance`; checked by grep.\n- [ ] The prompt instructs `criteria: [\"concurrency\"]`, forbids model-emitted severity, and states that an empty findings list is expected; checked by grep.\n- [ ] `make lint` passes (no Python changes expected; the prompt-loader test suite still passes: `pytest tests/unit -k reviewer`).","id":"cfdb-8b77-f63d-4c58","parent_id":"292f-57d1-e554-486f","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Author the code-review-concurrency Pass-1 overlay prompt (lockset-first quartet rubric)"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787250750474534001,"uuid":"09529066-63f8-4d86-a872-20a2f6373c6b"}